#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# bilateral_laplace_transform

#% text
# `bilateral_laplace_transform(f, t, s)` gives the two-sided Laplace transform `integrate(f*exp(-s*t), [t, -inf, inf])` of `f`.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# By default the value comes with its strip of convergence, as `conditional_expression(F, a < re(s) and re(s) < b)`; `generate_conditions: false` gives the value alone.

#% item
# Sums are transformed term by term; terms with undefined functions stay as transforms.

#% item
# `dirac_delta(t - a)` gives `exp(-a s)`; other integrands go to `integrate`.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
bilateral_laplace_transform(exp(-abs(t)), t, s)
#% output
#= conditional_expression(-2/(-1 + s^2), -1 < re(s) and re(s) < 1)

#% cell
bilateral_laplace_transform(unit_step(t), t, s)
#% output
#= conditional_expression(s^-1, re(s) > 0)

#% subsection
# Scope

#% text
# A Gaussian:

#% cell
bilateral_laplace_transform(exp(-t^2), t, s)
#% output
#= exp((1/4)*s^2)*pi^(1/2)

#% text
# A delta function:

#% cell
bilateral_laplace_transform(dirac_delta(t - c), t, s)
#% output
#= exp(-c*s)

#% text
# Linearity:

#% cell
bilateral_laplace_transform(a*f(t) + b*exp(-t^2), t, s)
#% output
#= a*bilateral_laplace_transform(f(t), t, s) + b*exp((1/4)*s^2)*pi^(1/2)

#% subsection
# Options

#% cell
bilateral_laplace_transform(exp(-abs(t)), t, s, generate_conditions: false)
#% output
#= -2/(-1 + s^2)

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `inverse_bilateral_laplace_transform`, `laplace_transform`, `fourier_transform`
